ules uh  they're not proteins but they may not be  as  stringent uh depending on their  dissociation temperatures  thank you so much and the next one i  have  direct pcr sounds great but what are its  drawbacks  oh it's a good question um  so one thing to keep in mind with direct  pcr is that  you are not actually purifying dna which  is  which is good if you don't need it but  if you need a purified genomic dna for a  certain downstream application  then a direct pcn may not be  a good approach for you to use  uh the next thing is that a sample size  is important uh because if you add  too too little uh you may not get  any amplification the signal will be too  low  or if you add too much of your sample  it will inhibit the pcr reaction  we do have some guidelines  on our website uh how much  of samples to add depending on on the  sample type  and depending on on the protocols are  used for  direct pcr  wonderful i have two more questions here  um premier design  rules look complicated i've seen some  tools on the web  any that you would recommend  uh yes of course uh we recommend  using our three uh online tool called  oligoperfect designer the design rules  are built into the algorithm um  so you don't really need to think about  all those  rules and we also  recently updated its bulk ordering  function to simplify the ordering if you  have  many primer pairs  perfect and i have one more question  that we're going to wrap up with  do i need to measure fidelity of the  enzyme that i am using  uh no you don't have to measure a  fidelity of the enzyme that you  are using it is a really tedious process  and uh usually the enzyme suppliers  do that for you  wonderful thank you again marie and kyle  for your time today and your important  research  we would also like to thank lab roots  and our sponsor thermo fisher scientific  for underwriting today's educational  webcast  before we go i would like to thank the  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting