ure  fidelity  and the results can vary significantly  there are three commonly used methods to  measure fidelity  that are shown here on the slide so on  the left there is a  colony screening assay  anger sequencing in the middle and on  the right next generation sequencing  so when comparing fidelity of different  polymerases  it is important to note the method that  was used  and only compare values that were  measured using the same methodology  and best if measured under the same  conditions  now colonies creating assays and sanger  sequencing both require  a huge amount of data to estimate  statistically significant  and reliable error rates of high  fidelity polymerases  nowadays next generation sequencing  enables analysis of large amounts of  data  and simplifies quantification of  statistical data  in this example in the graph fidelity of  dna polymerases was measured by  next generation sequencing  the values were normalized to tags any  polymerase  so here the value indicates how many  times  fidelity of the proofreading dna  polymerase is  higher compared to tac  measurements for these polymerases  indicated in the graph  were made side by side under the same  conditions  allowing accurate comparison of the  results  and let's look at how high-fidelity  enzymes can improve your lab  productivity  if you do cloning or sequencing any  sequence accuracy is critical  you want to minimize possibility of  having an  error in the pcr sequence and this is  where it is critical  to use a high fidelity polymerase  if you are to choose a pcr enzyme with  the highest fidelity possible  that will increase your confidence in  the dna sequence accuracy after  amplification  and save and save your time from  troubleshooting  and repetition of the experiment  we will now look into pcr enzymes with  high processivity and  how it can simplify pcr when working  with challenging samples  as was mentioned before processivity is  defined as the number of  nucleotides incorporated by the enzyme  in a single binding event to the dna  template  the higher the processivity the more  nucleotides the enzyme can incorporate  without  falling off the strand this often leads  to faster synthesis rate  and increased enzyme affinity to the  substrate  so how can a higher processivity of an  enzyme help  in a daily pcr  if you amplify dna from inhibitor rich  samples  like plants blood or soil  you may have difficulty with your pcr  this is typical because  pcr inhibitors are not completely  removed during  dna purification in such case  if you use highly processive enzyme you  can come  you you can overcome pcr inhibition  because these polymerases tolerate  sub-optimal reaction conditions  you can see an example here shown on the  right  where dna samples were amplified with  spiked  known pcr inhibitors using highly  processive platinum to attack dna  polymerase  it is often a challenge to amplify long  dna fragments  for example longer than 10 kilobase  success rates often low there is high  error rate and long  cycling protocol in such situations  high fidelity enzyme with high  processivity can simplify the workflow  because the enzyme ensures low error  rate and better amplification of longer  fragments  while high processivity of the enzyme  leads to  shorter cycling time and also increases  success rate  of the amplification overall  uh the gel image on the right side  shows amplification of 20 kilobases from  human genomic dna and  even up to 40 kilobases from e-coli  genomic dna  using our high-fidelity platinum  super phyto dna polymerase  gc rich dna amplification is another  common challenge in pcr  gc rich regions are harder to separate  and we can form secondary structures  after the separation most often  additives like dmso  are used to overcome these challenges  also there are specialized enhancers  available with some enzymes  that simplify reaction optimization  now our latest platinum superfine 2  enzyme  comes with an innovat