  also plays a critical role in driving  pcr  success  it is important to select a thermal  cycler  that ensures precise temperature control  thermal cyclers that deliver  inconsistent temperatures  can impact the accuracy of pcr results  especially if seeking a specific  annealing temperature  or performing pcr optimization protocols  this can lead to confusing results and  require  additional time to re-optimize protocols  to overcome this  one way to achieve the solution is to  look for a thermal cycler that provides  precise temperature control  a traditional gradient temperature  control  allows you to set a minimum temperature  on one side of the block  and a maximum temperature on the other  side  heat then transfers across the block in  a gradient but it lacks temperature  accuracy at any specific  well there are technologies available  that allow for precise temperature  control  the veriflex block technology  temperature control technology  utilizes blocks constructed of metal  segments each segment contains its own  heating and cooling elements depending  on the instrument  you can find either three or six  independent temperature zones  with the ability to uniquely control  each zone  you're able to achieve accurate  temperature control  which is ideal for enzyme studies  restriction digest  or any workflow requiring temperature  precision  if your lab is limited with the number  of thermal cyclers available  this can create backlogs and scheduling  and delays  and time to results today there are  thermal cyclers with very basic  features and small footprints  available at a reasonable price  this makes it easy to obtain  personalized instrumentation  allowing more lab members to have access  to their own benchtop thermal cyclers  one of the challenges many researchers  face when switching to a new model of  thermal cycler  is the ability to transition protocols  to the new instrument  without needing to re-optimize  experiments  thermal cyclers can operate using  different ramp rates  this makes it challenging to replicate  experimental results  across instruments to solve for this  look for thermal cyclers that offer  simulation mode  simulation mode provides a library of  instrument modes  that mimic the ramp rate of those  instruments  this makes it easy to transition  protocols and workflows  without the need to re-optimize  simulation mode is available on applied  biosystems thermal cyclers  another way to improve pcr efficiency  is having the ability to manage pcr  protocols and runs  while away from the lab look for  instruments that offer secure remote  access  with a wi-fi or cloud-enabled instrument  securely connect through your mobile  device or desktop computer  to start or stop a run design and share  protocols  or export data  this gives you greater control over your  research  whether you are in the lab or working  remotely  one area to improve pcr efficiency  that is often overlooked is the  selection of  optimal pcr plastics to fit your  instruments  thermal cycler manufacturers design  thermal blocks  and instrument lids to fit either a  limited  or wide range of pcr plastics  plastics that don't fit the thermal  block can lead to deformation  or crushing causing sample of aspiration  or loss of results  to solve this use the manufacturer  recommended plastics designed to fit  your pcr  or qpcr instrument  you can find recommended plastics by  using manufacturer compatibility tables  or use free online pcr plastic selection  guides  to confirm the fit of the plastic with  your specific instrument model  at the bottom of the slide you will find  a link to an online plastic selection  guide  for plastic compatibility with applied  biosystems  and other instrument manufacturers  pcr tubes are available in a variety of  sizes and formats  how do you select the right fit  first you need to know the profile of  your thermal block  are you working with a standard 0.2 mil  block  or a 0.1 mil also known as a fast  thermal block the tubes are different  sizes  to fit the appropriate block style  so make sure you select and know the  block format that you're working with  for the best fit  the second consideration is what style  cap do you need  domed caps are used in endpoint pcr  and can help absorb lid pressure and  prevent tube crushing  in instruments with inner concave lids  flat caps are recommended for  instruments that have a flat lid  and ultra clear cap are used when  working with real-time pcr  instruments  for experiments that require additional  throughput  we recommend the use of tube strips  dupe strips are available with either  eight tubes or twelve tubes  you can find tube strips that come with  tubes and caps separately  or to prevent cross-contamination we  recommend using  tube strips with attached cap  this will allow you to independently  open and close  each tube as you're working with that  particular sample  again use compatibility tables  or online selection guides to make sure  that the plastic is the right  fit for your thermal cycler  pcr plate selection also depends on the  instrument  lights come in a variety of styles they  can be non-skirted  semi-skirted or also known as  half-skirted  or skirted automation usually requires a  semi  or full skirted plate while non-skirted  plates  offer more flexibility to fit many  thermal block formats  the applied biosystems thermal cyclers  use  semi-skirted plates  deck style is another consideration  a flat deck offers the most universal  fit  with instrument lids and is also  compatible with  automated heat sealers while  raised decks are plates that have a  plastic lip that goes all the way around  the outer edge of the plate  this is required to help balance lid  pressure on certain thermal cyclers  and is required to fit with certain  certain thermal cycler lids  finally when should you seal with caps  versus films calf strips can be used for  both  tubes and plates they must fit securely  and seal tightly in order to avoid  evaporation  film is often used when working with 96  or 384 well plates  and film has the advantage that it can  be cut into smaller sizes  to fit segmented plates containing 24  32 or 48 wells  adhesive clear film or adhesive oil  can be used in endpoint pcr  while optically clear film is  recommended for real-time instruments  optically clear film is a pressure  sensitive film  and requires pressure to be applied to  form a proper seal  for more information about forming a  proper seal we do recommend the use of  an adhesive film  applicator when using the applicator you  apply  pressure gently and evenly across  the rims of every well to ensure that  the adhesive  deals with the well to prevent any  sample loss  other sealing considerations may include  considering the deck of the plate  especially if you're wanting to use an  automated ceiling method system  or the type of lettering do you need  raised or engraved lettering  to ensure you get a proper seal  for more information about any of the  pcr workflow topics  we have discussed we have assembled some  resources  we have a pcr workflow solution brochure  that goes through various portions of  the pcr workflow  you can also find more information on  our product pages  for our pcr enzymes thermal cyclers  or pcr plastics you can click the link  to get access to our pcr application  notes  we also have a pcr education webpage  that will discuss considerations for pcr  as well as common troubleshooting tips  links to our various brochures and  handbooks  will help further your understanding of  the pcr workflow  and the products available to support  your research  our hope is that this webinar provided  you with helpful insight  to drive your lab efficiency with  powerful pcr tools  designed for speed simplicity and  accuracy  i thank you for your time today and now  marie and i  will be happy to take any questions  thank you maria and kyle for your  informative presenta