erases possess other activities as  well that are important  for pcr processivity  is a measure of of a number of  nucleotides incorporated by the enzyme  in a single binding event until the  enzyme dissociates  from the template this activity is also  related to the enzyme's affinity  or how tightly it binds to the template  high perceptivity of enzyme contributes  to faster dna synthesis  higher inhibitor tolerance and ability  to amplify  longer dna fragments  another property hostart  helps ensure higher specificity and  sensitivity  dna polymerases can be active at room  temperature  while polymerases with hot start  mechanisms  are not these enzymes are activated  during  denaturation step at high temperatures  ensuring that amplification begins only  in a thermocycler giving you  a specific amplification only  now let's take a look at how hot start  enzymes can help increase your lab  productivity  amplification of non-specific dna  fragments  is a common pcr issue it can be caused  by  room temperature reaction setup because  any polymerases are somewhat  active at room temperature this is a  problem as primers might bind to  non-specific targets and give rise to  unwanted unwanted pcr products  similar to what you see in lanes 2 and 3  of in the gel image on the right  this can be avoided if the reaction is  set up on ice but  still enzymes activity is not fully  inhibited  an easiest solution to avoid this is to  use a hot start enzyme which is modified  to be inactive at room temperature  the hotspot modifier it can be  antibody chemical antibody or aptomer  in any case the reaction has to be  heated first  to activate the enzyme remove the  modifier  and enable synthesis of the new dna  strand  way a hot start enzyme provides better  specificity and yield  in pcro which can be seen in the  fourth and fifth lanes in the gel image  on the right  if you are using a proofreading enzyme  blocking  three prime to five prime exonuclease  activity is  especially critical non-hot start  proofreading enzymes or  proof eating enzymes with suboptimal hot  start mechanism  can have lower sensitivity and  specificity  especially when working with high  complexity dna  like human genomic theory and this is  because the exonuclease  or the proofreading activity can degrade  primers or templates  even before the pcr starts and  as you can see an example in the image  on the right  to prevent this you can use a  proofreading enzyme with  stringent hot start mechanism that  blocks both  polymerase and proofreading activities  and this enables detection of low  concentration targets  and amplify specific bands  now if you set up pcr using liquid  handling or robotic platforms  stability of assembled reactions at room  temperature is critical to achieve  optimal results hot start modifications  of pcr enzymes makes this  benchtop stability possible and if you  use a stringent hot start  you can achieve stability for  up to 24 hours  as you can see in the gel image on the  right  the same result was obtained when pcr  was  ran after this setup and when it was  left  for 24 hours at room temperature  if you are looking for a hot start  enzyme our in vitrogen  platinum pcr enzymes are designed with  antibody based platinum hotstar  technology  these antibodies fully inhibit enzyme  activity until the  initial denaturation step which means  greater target yield sensitivity  benchtop stability  and ability to set up reactions at room  temperature  and now let's move on and talk about  high fidelity pcr enzymes  polymerases can be defined by the error  rate that they make  the error rate of an enzyme is defined  as  the number of incorrect nucleotides per  total nucleotides  incorporated it can range from one in  millions  for high fidelity enzymes to one in  thousands for low fidelity  if you compare error rates or or  fidelity information for different dna  polymerases  it is important to keep in mind that  there are different ways to measure  fidelity  and the results can vary significantly  there are three commonly used methods to  measure fidelity  that are shown here on the slide so on  the left there is a  colony screening assay  anger sequencing in the middle and on  the right next generation sequencing  so when comparing fidelity of different  polymerases  it is important to note the method that  was used  and only compare values that were  measured using the same methodology  and best if measured under the same  conditions  now colonies creating assays and sanger  sequencing both require  a huge amount of data to estimate  statistically significant  and reliable error rates of high  fidelity polymerases  nowadays next generation sequencing  enables analysis of large amounts of  data  and simplifies quantification of  statistical data  in this example in the graph fidelity of  dna polymerases was measured by  next generation sequencing  the values were normalized to tags any  polymerase  so here the value indicates how many  times  fidelity of the proofreading dna  polymerase is  higher compared to tac  measurements for these polymerases  indicated in the graph  were made side by side under the same  conditions  allowing accurate comparison of the  results  and let's look at how high-fidelity  enzymes can improve your lab  productivity  if you do cloning or sequencing any  sequence accuracy is critical  you want to minimize possibility of  having an  error in the pcr sequence and this is  where it is critical  to use a high fidelity polymerase  if you are to choose a pcr enzyme with  the highest fidelity possible  that will increase your confidence in  the dna sequence accuracy after  amplification  and save and save your time from  troubleshooting  and repetition of the experiment  we will now look into pcr enzymes with  high processivity and  how it can simplify pcr when working  with challenging samples  as was mentioned before processivity is  defined as the number of  nucleotides incorporated by the enzyme  in a single binding event to the dna  template  the higher the processivity the more  nucleotides the enzyme can incorporate  without  falling off the strand this often leads  to faster synthesis rate  and increased enzyme affinity to the  substrate  so how can a higher processivity of an  enzyme help  in a daily pcr  if you amplify dna from inhibitor rich  samples  like plants blood or soil  you may have difficulty with your pcr  this is typical because  pcr inhibitors are not completely  removed during  dna purification in such case  if you use highly processive enzyme you  can come  you you can overcome pcr inhibition  because these polymerases tolerate  sub-optimal reaction conditions  you can see an example here shown on the  right  where dna samples were amplified with  spiked  known pcr inhibitors using highly  processive platinum to attack dna  polymerase  it is often a challenge to amplify long  dna fragments  for example longer than 10 kilobase  success rates often low there is high  error rate and long  cycling protocol in such situations  high fidelity enzyme with high  processivity can simplify the workflow  because the enzyme ensures low error  rate and better amplification of longer  fragments  while high processivity of the enzyme  leads to  shorter cycling time and also increases  success rate  of the amplification overall  uh the gel image on the right side  shows amplification of 20 kilobases from  human genomic dna and  even up to 40 kilobases from e-coli  genomic dna  using our high-fidelity platinum  super phyto dna polymerase  gc rich dna amplification is another  common challenge in pcr  gc rich regions are harder to separate  and we can form secondary structures  after the separation most often  additives like dmso  are used to overcome these challenges  also there are specialized enhancers  available with some enzymes  that simplify reaction optimization  now our latest platinum superfine 2  enzyme  comes with an innovative buffer that  allows amplification of gc rich targets  without a need for an enhancer bringing  down  all the optimization efforts to a  minimum  and in the next section i will talk  about some  some other ways uh how to increase  efficiency  when doing pcr  and one way is to choose direct pcr  approach  which enables pcr without prior dna  purification  cutting down the workflow time  significantly  the conventional pcr involves dna  purification  which can take from an hour to several  hours  depending on the method direct pcr does  not require this  and you can get from sample to pcr in  less than an hour in addition  their fpcr is handy when working with  very small samples as it requires  minimal handling and minimizes chances  to lose  the sample completely  it is also an ideal choice when working  with many samples in in high throughput  labs  for example for genotyping experiments  now the needing temperature for primers  often needs optimization to help ensure  efficient binding of the primers  to achieve the best result  it is a long and tedious process  especially if you work with  multiple primer sets however  our latest platinum pcr enzymes  are formulated with universal primer  annealing  temperature these enzymes allow you to  use  60 degrees annealing temperature for  different primer sets you can see  an example on the right where  all all dna fragments were amplified  at 60 degrees while their actual  calculated melting temperatures are  ranges from  59 to 69 degrees  this universal annealing feature  provides  two additional benefits shown here  first you can amplify multiple different  length fragments  in the same run or using the same  60 degrees annealing temperature and  using extension time  of the longest fragment  as depicted in the image on the top  fragments up to five kilobases can be  amplified without  losing specificity or yield  what this also means is that  different pcr assays can be cycled  together  using one cent thermocycler as shown in  the bottom image  typically with other enzymes different  pcr assays need to be run sequentially  or on different instruments so really  universal  primary annealing significantly saves  time  when it comes to reaction preparation  and also a cycling step  and lastly i will just briefly summarize  how to choose a right enzyme  based on your application  if you need to detect a target sequence  you basically get a yes or no answer  or use a low fidelity enzyme  for example platinum two tags in a  polymerase  now if you need to preserve dna sequence  accuracy  in applications like cloning with  sequencing or mutagenesis  high fidelity enzymes like platinum  superfight 2  will benefit you if you want  to detect target dna from samples  without purifying  dna choose platinum direct pci universal  master mix  because that will save you a lot of your  time  and down at the bottom there is a link  where you can find more information  about our  platinum enzyme family  and now primers primers are essential  for pcr  so let's look at some tips how to  simplify your pcr by  designing good primers  if primers were not designed correctly  you will either get  no amplification or lots of non-specific  products it is essential to follow  primer design rules to design primer for  optimal results here on the right  you can see several tips to keep in mind  when designing primers  that is a primer length gc content  number of dinucleotides or repetitive  bases  these are just some of the aspects to  consider when  designing a new primer pair or  you can use the specialized tools for  private design  where you do not really need to think  about these rules  and one example would be  a oligoperfect primer designer  online tool  and now my colleague kyle will continue  talking about thermocyclers and  plastics thank you very much maria for  all that wonderful information  beyond the selection of appropriate  reagents and primers to improve  pcr efficiency instrumentation  also plays a critical role in driving  pcr  success  it is important to select a thermal  cycler  that ensures precise temperature control  thermal cyclers that deliver  inconsistent temperatures  can impact the accuracy of pcr results  especially if seeking a specific  annealing temperature  or performing pcr optimization protocols  this can lead to confusing results and  require  additional time to re-optimize protocols  to overcome this  one way to achieve the solution is to  look for a thermal cycler that provides  precise temperature control  a traditional gradient temperature  control  allows you to set a minimum temperature  on one side of the block  and a maximum temperature on the other  side  heat then transfers across the block in  a gradient but it lacks temperature  accuracy at any specific  well there are technologies available  that allow for precise temperature  control  the veriflex block technology  temperature control technology  utilizes blocks constructed of metal  segments each segment contains its own  heating and cooling elements depending  on the instrument  you can find either three or six  independent temperature zones  with the ability to uniquely control  each zone  you're able to achieve accurate  temperature control  which is ideal for enzyme studies  restriction digest  or any workflow requiring temperature  precision  if your lab is limited with the number  of thermal cyclers available  this can create backlogs and scheduling  and delays  and time to results today there are  thermal cyclers with very basic  features and small footprints  available at a reasonable price  this makes it easy to obtain  personalized instrumentation  allowing more lab members to have access  to their own benchtop thermal cyclers  one of the challenges many researchers  face when switching to a new model of  thermal cycler  is the ability to transition protocols  to the new instrument  without needing to re-optimize  experiments  thermal cyclers can operate using  different ramp rates  this makes it challenging to replicate  experimental results  across instruments to solve for this  look for thermal cyclers that offer  simulation mode  simulation mode provides a library of  instrument modes  that mimic the ramp rate of those  instruments  this makes it easy to transition  protocols and workflows  without the need to re-optimize  simulation mode is available on applied  biosystems thermal cyclers  another way to improve pcr efficiency  is having the ability to manage pcr  protocols and runs  while away from the lab look for  instruments that offer secure remote  access  with a wi-fi or cloud-enabled instrument  securely connect through your mobile  device or desktop computer  to start or stop a run design and share  protocols  or export data  this gives you greater control over your  research  whether you are in the lab or working  remotely  one area to improve pcr efficiency  that is often overlooked is the  selection of  optimal pcr plastics to fit your  instruments  thermal cycler manufacturers design  thermal blocks  and instrument lids to fit either a  limited  or wide range of pcr plastics  plastics that don't fit the thermal  block can lead to deformation  or crushing causing sample of aspiration  or loss of results  to solve this use the manufacturer  recommended plastics designed to fit  your pcr  or qpcr instrument  you can find recommended plastics by  using manufacturer compatibility tables  or use free online pcr plastic selection  guides  to confirm the fit of the plastic with  your specific instrument model  at the bottom of the slide you will find  a link to an online plastic selection  guide  for plastic compatibility with applied  biosystems  and other instrument manufacturers  pcr tubes are available in a variety of  sizes and formats  how do you select the right fit  first you need to know the profile of  your thermal block  are you working with a standard 0.2 mil  block  or a 0.1 mil also known as a fast  thermal block the tubes are different  sizes  to fit the appropriate block style  so make sure you select and know the  block format that you're working with  for the best fit  the second consideration is what style  cap do you need  domed caps are used in endpoint pcr  and can help absorb lid pressure and  prevent tube crushing  in instruments with inner concave lids  flat caps are recommended for  instruments that have a flat lid  and ultra clear cap are used when  working with real-time pcr  instruments  for experiments that require additional  throughput  we recommend the use of tube strips  dupe strips are available with either  eight tubes or twelve tubes  you can find tube strips that come with  tubes and caps separately  or to prevent cross-contamination we  recommend using  tube strips with attached cap  this will allow you to independently  open and close  each tube as you're working with that  particular sample  again use compatibility tables  or online selection guides to make sure  that the plastic is the right  fit for your thermal cycler  pcr plate selection also depends on the  instrument  lights come in a variety of styles they  can be non-skirted  semi-skirted or also known as  half-skirted  or skirted automation usually requires a  semi  or full skirted plate while non-skirted  plates  offer more flexibility to fit many  thermal block formats  the applied biosystems thermal cyclers  use  semi-skirted plates  deck style is another consideration  a flat deck offers the most universal  fit  with instrument lids and is also  compatible with  automated heat sealers while  raised decks are plates that have a  plastic lip that goes all the way around  the outer edge of the plate  this is required to help balance lid  pressure on certain thermal cyclers  and is required to fit with certain  certain thermal cycler lids  finally when should you seal with caps  versus films calf strips can be used for  both  tubes and plates they must fit securely  and seal tightly in order to avoid  evaporation  film is often used when working with 96  or 384 well plates  and film has the advantage that it can  be cut into smaller sizes  to fit segmented plates containing 24  32 or 48 wells  adhesive clear film or adhesive oil  can be used in endpoint pcr  while optically clear film is  recommended for real-time instruments  optically clear film is a pressure  sensitive film  and requires pressure to be applied to  form a proper seal  for more information about forming a  proper seal we do recommend the use of  an adhesive film  applicator when using the applicator you  apply  pressure gently and evenly across  the rims of every well to ensure that  the adhesive  deals with the well to prevent any  sample loss  other sealing considerations may include  considering the deck of the plate  especially if you're wanting to use an  automated ceiling method system  or the type of lettering do you need  raised or engraved lettering  to ensure you get a proper seal  for more information about any of the  pcr workflow topics  we have discussed we have assembled some  resources  we have a pcr workflow solution brochure  that goes through various portions of  the pcr workflow  you can also find more information on  our product pages  for our pcr enzymes thermal cyclers  or pcr plastics you can click the link  to get access to our pcr application  notes  we also have a pcr education webpage  that will discuss considerations for pcr  as well as common troubleshooting tips  links to our various brochures and  handbooks  will help further your understanding of  the pcr workflow  and the products available to support  your research  our hope is that this webinar provided  you with helpful insight  to drive your lab efficiency with  powerful pcr tools  designed for speed simplicity and  accuracy  i thank you for your time today and now  marie and i  will be happy to take any questions  thank you maria and kyle for your  informative presentation  we will now start the live q a portion  of the webinar  if you have any questions you'd like to  ask please do so now just click  on the ask a question box located on the  far left of your screen  and we'll answer as many questions as we  have time for  let's get started our first question  do i still need to use the universal  annealing temperature  i trust annealing temperatures i  calculated  oh thank you for the first question um  the answer is uh no uh you don't have to  use universal  primary annealing temperature the  enzymes  will work with the calculated  temperatures as well  but the universal kneeling feature is  convenient when  you are working with multiple primer  sets  wonderful the next question i have how  do i get samples of the enzymes you  mentioned  i thank you for the question uh yes so  to get the samples please contact  our thermo fisher sales representatives  and in some countries you can also  request the  product samples directly on our website  wonderful the next question i have what  type of pcr plastics do you recommend  if i am automating some of my workflow  with a robot  that's a great question there are  actually two factors to consider when  thinking about using plastics for  robotics  the first one will be the skirt type as  i mentioned before  semi-skirted and fully skirted plastics  are usually recommended for automation  that's important because the robotic  grippers or paddles  that move the plates around will need  some surface in order to do their  gripping  the second consideration is the pcr  plastic material  some plates are prone to warping and  cannot work well within automation  automated processes therefore we would  recommend  plates that are constructed with a  stronger polycarbonate frame  which resists distortion caused by those  robotic grippers  and tolerates the robotic transfer well  those plates still contain the same thin  walled polypropylene wells that are  traditional in all pcr  plastics but that frame makes an  important difference  and for thermo fisher scientific we have  both  the endura plate and the armadillo  options for polycarbonate frame plastics  thank you so much the next one i have  do your thermal cyclers have  gradient capability  great question the proflex  verity pro and simply amp thermal  cyclers  feature our veriflex block technology so  this is that precise  temperature control the instrument  either contains three or six segmented  metal blocks  each with their own heating and cooling  element so that each zone  can be set with a unique and independent  temperature  our verity pro instrument also has the  ability  to set temperature based on the minimum  and maximum temperature across  the block or to set the midpoint which  is our zone three range of the thermal  block  thank you so much the next one i have  fidelity value that you showed in the  graph  does look different from what i see on  my enzymes manual can you explain that  that's a great question thank you  yes correct fidelity values  can different it can differ  depending on the methods used in  measuring and so  different companies  will show different values for their  products  as it was mentioned during the webinar  um for example  if values were measured using the lack  based method it will vary significantly  compared to  values measured using next generation  sequencing  and this is especially true for  for higher fidelity enzymes where you  really need  to use a sensitive mesh method to  measure fidelity  accurately uh the bottom line though is  when comparing fidelity values of  different enzymes  they should be measured side by side  using the same method  under the same conditions  wonderful thank you we have time for a  few more here  do you have direct kit for rna  yes we do have a direct kit for rna  we recently launched a new kit it is  called  superscript for cells direct  it synthesizes the cdna directly from  rna of cell samples  thank you another question tell me more  about wireless connectivity  yes cloud enabled instruments allow you  the freedom to design  and securely upload your methods monitor  runs or check instrument availability  from  any mobile device or desktop computer  you simply need to look for an  instrument that's wi-fi or cloud enabled  and connect to it  great and how can i prevent sample  evaporation during pcr  great question proper sealing of your  pcr reactions  is essential to preventing evaporation  and sample loss  if you're using an adhesive film you  want to make sure that it properly fits  the size of the plate  and to align the cover across all wells  adhesive film will stick an optical film  does require  it's pressure sensitive and requires  pressure but in both cases we do  recommend the use of the adhesive  applicator tool  to firmly press across the entire film  and to make sure that the rims of  every well are securely connected and  sealed  you can also use cap strips with plates  so if you're not getting a proper seal  that's another option is to consider  using cap strips  as long as you ensure that the cap strip  has a tight fit  in with the plate so those are a few  suggestions on how to properly seal  thank you okay let's see here the next  one i have  can the choice of hot start modifier  make a difference  well that's a great question thank you  now my short answer would be  yes there is a difference and it mainly  depends  on your application so for example  antibody modifiers are quite stringent  and can fully inactivate the enzymes  but antibodies are proteins so they not  mean so they may not be suitable if your  application  requires low protein concentrations  uh chemical modification  modifies the enzyme so the enzyme  that is that has a chemical hot stars  may not be  fully reactivated during the activation  step  which quite often impacts the  amplification  of log sequences and sometimes enzyme  sensitivity and  then antibody and aptamer  so these are small molecules uh  they're not proteins but they may not be  as  stringent uh depending on their  dissociation temperatures  thank you so much and the next one i  have  direct pcr sounds great but what are its  drawbacks  oh it's a good question um  so one thing to keep in mind with direct  pcr is that  you are not actually purifying dna which  is  which is good if you don't need it but  if you need a purified genomic dna for a  certain downstream application  then a direct pcn may not be  a good approach for you to use  uh the next thing is that a sample size  is important uh because if you add  too too little uh you may not get  any amplification the signal will be too  low  or if you add too much of your sample  it will inhibit the pcr reaction  we do have some guidelines  on our website uh how much  of samples to add depending on on the  sample type  and depending on on the protocols are  used for  direct pcr  wonderful i have two more questions here  um premier design  rules look complicated i've seen some  tools on the web  any that you would recommend  uh yes of course uh we recommend  using our three uh online tool called  oligoperfect designer the design rules  are built into the algorithm um  so you don't really need to think about  all those  rules and we also  recently updated its bulk ordering  function to simplify the ordering if you  have  many primer pairs  perfect and i have one more question  that we're going to wrap up with  do i need to measure fidelity of the  enzyme that i am using  uh no you don't have to measure a  fidelity of the enzyme that you  are using it is a really tedious process  and uh usually the enzyme suppliers  do that for you  wonderful thank you again marie and kyle  for your time today and your important  research  we would also like to thank lab roots  and our sponsor thermo fisher scientific  for underwriting today's educational  webcast  before we go i would like to thank the  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting